                ("box", "why", "Why · the muscle claims the extra flow", "Working muscle consumes O₂ and releases <strong>metabolites</strong> (CO₂, H⁺, K⁺, adenosine, lactate). These <strong>dilate its own arterioles</strong>, lowering resistance and raising flow (Week 7 local regulation). At the same time exercise <strong>increases sympathetic outflow</strong>, which constricts vessels elsewhere (gut, kidney), but <strong>skeletal-muscle vessels respond weakly to sympathetic drive</strong>, so local dilation wins there. The result is in slide 75: cardiac output rises ~<strong>3.5-fold</strong> (<strong>5.0 → 17.5 L/min</strong>), and skeletal muscle flow rises from <strong>1030 mL/min (20%)</strong> to <strong>12 500 mL/min (73%)</strong>. Skin flow rises (430 → 1900 mL/min, for cooling), brain flow stays roughly constant, and kidney and gut flow fall (950 → 600 and 1200 → 600 mL/min)."),
