                ("box", "why", "Why · exercise pumps blood back", "A higher cardiac output needs a matching <strong>venous return</strong>. The <strong>skeletal-muscle pump</strong>: contracting muscles squeeze deep veins, and one-way valves push blood toward the heart. The <strong>respiratory pump</strong>: deeper, faster breathing lowers intrathoracic pressure on inspiration, drawing blood into the chest. More filling then increases stroke volume through <strong>Frank–Starling</strong>, and atrial stretch raises heart rate through the <strong>Bainbridge reflex</strong>. Both are positive feedback that magnifies the rise in output and pressure (Week 8)."),
                ("plate", 77, "The receptors engaged in exercise", "Proprioceptors, metaboreceptors, central/peripheral chemoreceptors, pain receptors, thermoreceptors"),
                ("box", "mnem", "Exam hook · what each receptor senses", "<strong>Body movement → proprioceptors</strong>. <strong>Local metabolites → metaboreceptors</strong> (in muscle). <strong>Circulating metabolites (CO₂, H⁺) → central and peripheral chemoreceptors</strong>. <strong>Pain → pain receptors</strong>. <strong>Body heat → thermoreceptors</strong> (§2). The list isn't exhaustive, not all are engaged in every type of exercise, and the response depends on the <strong>type and magnitude</strong> of exercise. Add the brain's own <strong>central command</strong> (feed-forward drive from the motor cortex), which raises heart rate and ventilation even before metabolites build up."),
