                ("box", "why", "Why · the baroreflex is reset", "The <strong>baroreflex</strong> is negative feedback: a rise in arterial pressure leads to less sympathetic activity and more vagal activity, which lowers pressure again. During exercise, pressure <em>needs</em> to rise to push flow through working muscle. If the reflex stayed at its resting setting it would fight the response. Instead the curve <strong>resets rightward and upward</strong>: the reflex still works, but around a <strong>higher operating pressure</strong>, so it is <strong>less sensitive for the same blood pressure</strong> (a given MAP now produces <em>more</em> sympathetic activity). Central command and muscle afferents drive the resetting. The baroreflex keeps buffering moment-to-moment swings, just around a new set point."),
